<body>
  <GameNav />
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &gt; <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Rooms &amp; Exits</a> &gt; <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Chapter 2</a> &gt; Level 16</nav>

  <main class="seo-section">
    <h1>Rooms &amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 16 Walkthrough: Art Supplies Store</h1>

    <div class="disclaimer">Unofficial fan-made guide. Not affiliated with Webelinx Games, the developer of Rooms &amp; Exits: Escape Room Games.</div>

    <div class="verify-note">Rebuilt frame by frame from two separate recorded playthroughs — a full 8 minute 42 second run and a 5 minute 1 second run — extracted at one frame per second, with the lock panels re-extracted at four frames per second and read at full resolution. The three-digit drawer code was read from three independent frames in one recording and confirmed again in the second recording, and all four readings agree. The two colour locks were not readable twice at the level of individual colours, so this page explains how they work and does not print a colour order it could not verify. Where the footage cannot settle a detail, the page says so rather than guessing.</div>

    <LevelSelector chapter={2} current={16} variant="jump" label="Chapter 2 · No Honor Among Thieves" />

    <div class="answer-box">
      <span class="label">Quick answer</span>
      <p>The only number in the Art Supplies Store is <b>052</b>, and it goes into the three-window LCD panel on the white drawer face in the back of the shop. Finish worth <b>+50 coins</b>.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Read the <b>Art Book</b> in the front of the shop for the colour chart and the colour wheel — it is the reference for both colour locks.</li>
        <li>Take the <b>scissors</b> and the <b>brush</b> from the red pencil cup, and the <b>blue book</b> out of the green bookshelf.</li>
        <li>Open the <b>nine-colour safe</b> by pressing its colour buttons in the order the clue gives you → a <b>piece of paper</b>.</li>
        <li>Open the desk's <b>four-button drawer</b> the same way → <b>tape</b> and another <b>piece of paper</b>.</li>
        <li>Read the <b>brush rack</b> in the back corner: sixteen brushes, each on a numbered yellow label.</li>
        <li>Set the three-window panel to <b>0 · 5 · 2</b> → the drawer releases a <b>tempera tube</b> and a <b>torn piece of paper</b>.</li>
        <li>Work the <b>arrow panel</b> on the next drawer until it releases the <b>art palette</b> and a paint tube.</li>
        <li>Paint the <b>mandala canvas</b> on the easel: <b>red centre, blue clovers, yellow dots, white lines</b>.</li>
        <li>Hang the finished painting in the <b>picture frame</b> → four keys on hooks.</li>
        <li>Use the <b>heart-bow key</b> on the <b>pale blue door</b> → results screen, <b>+50 coins</b>.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="video-wrap">
      <iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/VWtYcORJ-qo" title="Rooms &amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 16 Art Supplies Store walkthrough" allowfullscreen loading="lazy"></iframe>
    </div>

    <img class="hero" src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l16-hero-art-store.jpg" alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 2 Level 16 Art Supplies Store: shelves of paint tubes sorted by colour, a central swatch table, a white counter with a computer and the glass EXIT doors" width="1200" height="594" />

    <h2>What this level actually is<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The Art Supplies Store is one of the biggest single rooms in Chapter 2, and it is a shop rather than a puzzle box. The floor is one open space with three distinct working areas. Down the left wall run two tall shelving units packed with paint tubes arranged as a rainbow gradient, blue and violet at one end, red at the other. In front of them sits a long display table covered in colour swatches laid out in the same spectrum. On the right is a white checkout counter with a computer showing a colour wheel, a keyboard, a mouse, a red pencil cup and a roll of tape, with a black office chair pushed under it. In the back corner stand an easel, an artist's mannequin hand, a brush rack and a couple of grey cabinets, and the back wall holds a glass double door marked EXIT with a potted plant beside it and a second, pale blue door further along.</p>
    <p>That layout is the reason people stall here. Nothing is signposted. There are no arrows, no obvious first object, and no locked door that announces the shape of the level. Instead there are six separate locks scattered across three areas, and the order you open them in is decided by what each one hands you: a piece of paper leads to another piece of paper, a brush rack leads to a number, a number leads to a paint tube, and the paint is the only thing that finishes the painting that opens the wall. Players who start at the counter and work left to right in a straight line will pass three of the six locks without noticing them.</p>
    <p>The door you leave through is the least dramatic object in the room. It is not the glass EXIT doors with the sparkles on the handle, and it is not the door behind the counter. It is the plain pale blue door on the back wall, the one with the lever handle and the keyhole, and it stays shut until you have a specific key out of a specific compartment. Everything in the room is upstream of that key.</p>
    <p>The recording we rebuilt this page from finishes the level in 8:28 of level time while stopping to open every drawer and read every readable prop. A player who knows the chain can do it far faster. A player who does not can spend twenty minutes in the same shop and never find the three-digit panel, because it is on the *back* of a drawer unit in a corner, at knee height, and it does not look like a lock until you are standing in front of it.</p>

    <h2>Step by step<span class="accent">.</span></h2>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 1 · 0:04</span>
      <strong>Read the room before you touch anything</strong>
      The first thing the level gives you is a wide shot with no zoom frame over it, so use it. Left: two paint shelves plus the swatch table. Right: the white counter, the computer, the chair, the tape. Back left: the easel, the mannequin hand and the brush rack. Back right: the glass EXIT doors, the plant, the pale blue door and the grey cabinets. Three of the six locks are in the back half of the room, which is why the front half feels empty: it is mostly scenery plus the counter props.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VWtYcORJ-qo&t=4s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:04</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 2 · 0:12</span>
      <strong>The red pencil cup: scissors and a brush</strong>
      On the checkout counter, beside a piece of paper with cut-out holes in it, sits a red metal cup holding a pair of green-handled scissors and a couple of brushes. Tap it and the game hands you the scissors first, then the brush. Both go into the bag and both matter later: the brush is what paints the canvas, and the scissors are part of the paper chain. The paper with the two holes punched in it is a prop — the recording opens it, reads it and never comes back.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l16-pencil-cup.jpg" alt="The red pencil cup on the checkout counter holding scissors and brushes, with a punched piece of paper beside it" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VWtYcORJ-qo&t=12s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:12</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 3 · 0:30</span>
      <strong>The Art Book is the colour reference for the whole room</strong>
      An art book lies open in the front of the shop, and it is the single most useful object in the room. The left-hand page is a grid of colour swatches, each one named — red, orange, yellow, green, blue, violet, blue, black, white and so on — and the right-hand page carries a colour wheel with paint pots and a brush beside it. This is the page that tells you what the game means by "blue" when a lock is asking for blue, and it is the reference the two colour-button locks are built around. Read it before you touch either panel; it costs a few seconds and saves a lot of blind pressing.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l16-art-book.jpg" alt="The open Art Book showing a named colour swatch chart on the left page and a colour wheel on the right" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VWtYcORJ-qo&t=30s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:30</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 4 · 2:24</span>
      <strong>The nine-colour safe panel</strong>
      The first real lock is a brushed-metal face with nine round colour buttons in a 3x3 grid and a dark LCD strip above them. It sits on a safe body in the shop, and it works on one rule: every press you make is added to the strip above the grid as a coloured dot, in order. That strip is your only feedback. Get the order right and the safe door swings open; get it wrong and the dots clear so you can start again. The colours on the nine buttons are not the puzzle — the order is.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l16-colour-safe-panel.jpg" alt="The nine-button colour safe panel with a 3x3 grid of coloured buttons and an LCD strip above them" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VWtYcORJ-qo&t=144s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:24</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 5 · 2:35</span>
      <strong>The green bookshelf and the one blue book</strong>
      Above the desk stands a white shelf packed with identical green folders or books, and exactly one of them is blue and pushed out of line. Tap it and it comes away as a book — but more importantly it is the level teaching you its own grammar: in this room, the odd object out is the interactive one. Once you have seen that, the brush rack with one empty slot and the drawer with a different handle stop looking like decoration.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l16-bookshelf-blue-book.jpg" alt="The white bookshelf of green books with a single blue book pushed out of line" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VWtYcORJ-qo&t=155s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:35</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 6 · 3:12</span>
      <strong>The four-button drawer on the white desk</strong>
      The desk in the back area has three drawers, and the top one wears a small white panel with four round buttons in a row. Unlike the nine-button safe, these buttons are not fixed colours: each tap cycles the button through a palette, so the same button can be red, then yellow, then green, then grey. The drawer releases when the four buttons are left sitting in the right four colours. You are not entering a sequence here, you are dialling in a set — which is why tapping the same button repeatedly is correct play and not a mistake.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l16-drawer-colour-buttons.jpg" alt="The four round colour buttons on the top drawer of the white desk, two of them lit red and yellow" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VWtYcORJ-qo&t=192s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:12</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 7 · 3:36</span>
      <strong>The brush rack: sixteen brushes, sixteen numbers</strong>
      In the back corner, above the counter that runs under the easel, hangs a white rack holding two rows of paintbrushes. Under every brush is a small yellow label with a number printed on it, and the numbers run well past the sizes a real brush would carry — which is the tell that they are a code and not a product label. Read the rack as a row of digits, in reading order, top row first. This is the rack that teaches the three-digit code in step 8.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l16-brush-rack-numbers.jpg" alt="The brush rack with two rows of paintbrushes, each above a yellow label printed with a number" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VWtYcORJ-qo&t=216s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:36</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 8 · 4:14</span>
      <strong>Set the three-window panel to 0 · 5 · 2</strong>
      The white drawer face in the back corner carries three square LCD windows with green seven-segment digits and a silver bar handle underneath. Each window is a cycler: tap it and the digit goes up by one, wrapping from 9 back to 0. Set the windows left to right to <b>0</b>, <b>5</b> and <b>2</b> and stop. Do not keep tapping to "confirm" — there is no confirm key, the drawer releases on its own the moment the three windows read 052.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l16-code-052.jpg" alt="The white drawer panel with three green LCD windows reading 0 5 2 and a silver handle below" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VWtYcORJ-qo&t=254s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4:14</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 9 · 4:35</span>
      <strong>What the 052 drawer holds</strong>
      The drawer slides out with two things in it: a red tube of tempera paint, labelled WATER COLOR / Tempera, and a torn piece of white paper. Take both. The tempera is one of the paint sources for the canvas, and the torn paper joins the papers you already have — the level keeps handing you fragments of the same sheet, and the finished sheet is what tells you how the mandala is coloured.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l16-tempera-drawer.jpg" alt="The opened drawer with a red tempera paint tube picked up and a torn piece of paper beside it" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VWtYcORJ-qo&t=275s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4:35</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 10 · 5:57</span>
      <strong>The arrow panel, and the palette behind it</strong>
      The next drawer down has a different face: a black window in the middle with a green left-arrow button and a green right-arrow button flanking it. Tapping the left button adds a left arrow to the window, tapping the right button adds a right arrow, and the window fills up as you go. You are building a short string of arrows, and the drawer releases when the string matches the one the room is asking for. Behind it is the <b>art palette</b>, a wooden palette loaded with daubs of paint, plus a paint tube. The palette is the thing that actually turns the canvas from an outline into a painting.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l16-arrow-panel.jpg" alt="The arrow drawer panel with left and right green arrow buttons and a five-arrow string in the black window" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VWtYcORJ-qo&t=357s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">5:57</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 11 · 6:31</span>
      <strong>The back corner: easel, mannequin hand, brush rack</strong>
      The back corner is its own little studio and it is easy to walk past because the camera gives you a wide shot of it rather than a zoom. There is an easel with a blank canvas on it, a wooden artist's mannequin hand on a small stand, the brush rack above, a shelf of paint bottles to the right and a green paint spill on the floor. Everything the painting needs is in this corner except the palette and the paint tubes, which come out of the two drawers you have just opened.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l16-easel-corner.jpg" alt="The back corner of the shop with the easel, the wooden mannequin hand, the brush rack and paint shelves" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VWtYcORJ-qo&t=391s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">6:31</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 12 · 7:43</span>
      <strong>Paint the mandala: red centre, blue clovers, yellow dots</strong>
      Tap the canvas on the easel and it fills the screen as a black-and-white mandala outline with the palette on the left and a brush below. Then colour it by shape, not by taste. The four oval petals that meet in the middle are <b>red</b>. The four large clover shapes sitting above, below, left and right of them are <b>blue</b>. The four small round dots in the corners between the clovers are <b>yellow</b>. The curved connecting lines and the background stay <b>white</b>. When the last region is filled the canvas is accepted and becomes an item you can carry.
      <table class="paint-map">
        <tr><th>Region</th><th>Colour</th><th>Where it sits</th></tr>
