    <h2>The chain, in order<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Six locks, one room, and no signposts. Written as a dependency map the level is much smaller than it feels:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Counter → tools.</b> The red pencil cup gives the scissors and the brush. The Art Book gives the colour vocabulary. Neither is gated.</li>
      <li><b>Colour clue → nine-button safe.</b> The safe's strip records your presses as coloured dots; match the order and it hands over a piece of paper.</li>
      <li><b>Colour clue → four-button drawer.</b> Dial each of the four buttons to the right colour and the top desk drawer gives tape and a second paper.</li>
      <li><b>Brush rack → 052.</b> The numbered labels under the brushes are the source of the three-digit code.</li>
      <li><b>052 → paint.</b> The drawer releases a tempera tube and a torn piece of paper. The arrow drawer next to it releases the art palette.</li>
      <li><b>Palette + brush → canvas.</b> Red centre, blue clovers, yellow dots, white lines. The finished painting is a carry item.</li>
      <li><b>Painting → frame → keys.</b> The frame slides aside and four keys appear. The heart-bow key is the one.</li>
      <li><b>Key → blue door → +50.</b> Nothing else gates the exit.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Looked at that way, the Art Supplies Store has exactly two authored puzzles — the colour locks and the painting — plus one arithmetic gate (the code) and one shape-matching gate (the key). Everything else in the room is a container. That is why the level takes eight minutes on a first run and about three on a second: the containers are all in corners, and each one only opens once you have carried something from a different corner to it.</p>
    <p>The trap is the order. If you open the two colour locks before reading the Art Book you will be pressing buttons with no reference and no way to tell a deliberate wrong answer from a bug. If you paint the canvas before finding the palette you will be looking at an outline with nothing to fill it. If you find the frame before the painting you will assume it is set dressing — which is exactly what it looks like.</p>

    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The three-digit panel is not in the front of the shop.</b> It is on the face of a drawer unit in the back corner, low down and half out of the default camera angle. Players who sweep the counter and the shelves and then leave conclude the level has no code at all.</li>
      <li><b>The digits cycle, they do not accept typing.</b> There is no keypad and no confirm button. You tap a window to increment it. Overshoot and you have to go all the way round through 9 back to 0, which is what makes the panel feel broken the first time.</li>
      <li><b>052 is a drawer code, not a door code.</b> The most common assumption in this level is that the three-digit number opens the exit. It does not. It opens a drawer with a paint tube in it, and the exit is opened by a key.</li>
      <li><b>The EXIT doors are a decoy.</b> The glass double doors with the sparkles on the handle are the shop's front doors. The door that ends the level is the plain pale blue one further along the same wall.</li>
      <li><b>The frame reads as decoration.</b> An empty frame on a wall in an art shop looks like set dressing. It is the last puzzle in the level and it hides the keys.</li>
      <li><b>The four keys look interchangeable.</b> Three of them are noise. The heart-shaped bow on the fourth matches the heart-shaped outline around the door's keyhole, and that is the only signal the game gives you.</li>
      <li><b>The canvas is coloured by shape, not by palette order.</b> The palette holds more colours than the painting uses. Painting everything you can reach does not finish it; the pattern has four regions and four answers.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The painting, in detail<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The mandala is the only puzzle in the level that is judged on a picture rather than on a number, so it is worth describing precisely. The outline is a square-ish rosette built around a four-petal centre. Four elongated oval petals meet at the middle of the canvas and overlap slightly, forming a cross. Around that cross sit four large clover shapes — each one a four-lobed cluster — positioned at twelve, three, six and nine o'clock. Between the clovers, in the four diagonal corners of the design, are four small round dots. Thin curved lines run from the centre out through the clovers and round to the dots, closing the whole pattern into a loop.</p>
    <p>The colours follow the structure exactly: <b>red</b> on the four centre petals, <b>blue</b> on the four clovers, <b>yellow</b> on the four corner dots, and nothing at all on the lines or the background, which stay white. That is why the painting is quick once you know it and slow when you do not: there are only four decisions to make, but the brush follows your finger, so a sloppy drag can spill one region into the next and leave the canvas unfinished without telling you which part is wrong.</p>
    <p>The reference for the palette comes from the paper chain. The room gives you torn and rolled pieces of paper from three different containers — one out of the nine-colour safe, one out of the four-button drawer, one out of the 052 drawer — and the recordings show them being combined in the inventory and examined alongside the colour guide. If you are stuck on the painting rather than on a lock, open the inventory and examine the paper items and the colour guide before you start mixing colours on the canvas.</p>

    <div class="warn">
      <b>What we could not verify:</b> the exact colour order for the two colour locks. Our footage shows both panels being worked and both containers opening, but the individual colours are small, the recording is compressed, and repeated reads of the same frame disagreed — so rather than print a sequence that might send you round in circles, this page explains the rule and stops there. The same applies to the arrow string on the drawer panel: we can see it being built and we can see the drawer release, but we could not read the finished string twice. What we did verify twice, and in two separate recordings, is the drawer code <b>052</b>, the contents of the drawers, the four colours of the mandala, the four keys behind the frame and the <b>+50 coins</b> reward.
    </div>

